Original commit body from d51308d1d64503972eb818b695ef10496fcaa3d1:

ADR-0061 ruling 3 says a catalogue file's tier is its own provenance line,
whatever path loaded it. The taint_*.d channels obeyed that; the
--taint-sources/--taint-sinks/--taint-sanitizers flags and a claims file's
extra_catalogs: did not. load_full_taint_catalog sent those paths straight
into user layers, so a file declaring provenance: community had its
sanitizers stamped user_supplied (clearing the flow: violated ->
confirmed_with_caveats, exit 3), survived --no-default-overlays, and its
sources/sinks could displace shipped rows. The report listed it as
USER-SUPPLIED in text while JSON tiers listed it as community.

Every taint path family now goes through one partition
(taint.split_community_files). Community files join the community layer:
they only add, their sanitizers are stamped community and withheld
(withheld_community_sanitizer), and include_community=False drops them.
verify-claims applies the same partition to its report: layers and
user_supplied hold only the operator's own files; a loaded community file is
listed under the new catalog_provenance.named_community_files (plus a text
note and a stderr line). Under --no-default-overlays it is listed under
omitted_community_overlays with its family, along with any community file
in a taint channel (which tiers had listed as loaded), and a stderr notice
reusing the overlay one.

Reading the provenance line comes before the real load, so it now raises
TaintCatalogError on an unparseable file instead of a raw YAMLError (this
was already latent for channel files).

Spec ADR-0061 status row and docs/CATALOGUES.md updated.

